[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Main Modules

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**HVPS Communication** – The HVPS Interface interfaces with the HVPS (High Voltage Power Supply)
device itself. This interface firmware automatically controls the operation of the HVPS and monitors
feedback signals to automatically adjust output or respond to critical events such as over-temperatures
and other faults.

**Control Loop** – The control loop monitors feedback signals to automatically adjust the output and create
a stable X-Ray emission.

**Main Control Communication** – The HVPS Interface will receive both serial data signals and signal
status flags from the main control firmware. Serial data comes via RS485 protocol and signal flags by
digital logic which are ultimately interpreted using the HAL Libraries for UART and GPIO. These signals
will indicate instructions for the HVPS to run as well as fault conditions, such as interlocks and
over-temperatures. The HVPS firmware will periodically report data back to the main control firmware
approximately once per second.

**Setpoints** – discrete data points directed by the main control input for the HVPS firmware to output

**Feedback Reporting** – the HVPS firmware monitors cathode, filament, the grid, arcs, HV status, and
the Master fault and reports them back to the Main Control firmware.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
PREFIX: SW-H1FWHVPS-
TITLE: Design Items

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-106
TITLE: Bootloader Communication
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware communicates with the PC via one of the microcontrollers' serial UART interface. The
firmware both receives and transmits data on this UART port. Due to the nature of UART having
independent transmit and receive lines, the transmit and receive functionality operate
independently. As the UART baud rate is relatively slow compared to the processing speed of the
microcontroller, both sending and receiving data is performed with non-blocking interrupt callback
functions.

Transmitted data is sent as a buffer of bytes, representing a singular data packet to the PC, with
the interrupt callback occurring once all data bytes have been transmitted. The firmware sets a flag
when transmission is initiated, and clears the flag after transmission is finished, to not create
transmission conflicts on the transmit line.

Reception is performed one byte at a time, with the interrupt callback occurring once a data byte is
available. The parsing of the received byte is done to determine whether a complete data packet has
been received from the PC, as according to the communication protocol. Once a completed data packet
has been received, the firmware sets a flag for further data processing outside of the parsing
inside of the interrupt callback.

Each recognized command has a fixed expected frame length. After the five-character command header
is received, the callback uses that command's byte count to delimit the frame rather than treating a
newline-valued byte in a binary payload as an early terminator. An unrecognized header or a receive
buffer limit resets the parser. The callback always re-arms one-byte interrupt reception, including
while a complete frame awaits deferred processing; bytes received while processing is pending are
discarded, and reception remains available for a subsequent valid frame.

When deferred processing accepts the seven-byte `*B00TL\n` frame, it erases the single application
CRC flash page at `CRC_ADDR_START` and invokes a system reset so execution enters the bootloader.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-19
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-107
TITLE: Internal ADC
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware reads several feedback signals from the internal ADC peripheral. 15 of the ADC channels
are used, and they are read sequentially using the manufacturer's built in hardware abstraction
library for DMA reads of the ADC peripheral. The firmware periodically reads newly received values
before initiating a new DMA read.

The internal ADC DMA buffer is polled by the application. Half-transfer and transfer-complete
interrupts are disabled because the buffer does not require callback processing and continuous
interrupt activity could interfere with the external ADC DMA transfers. DMA transfer-error handling
remains enabled.
<<<
