  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-110
TITLE: External ADC Communication
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware communicates with two external ADC IC chips. Each chip has one channel, and they are
responsible for reading the mA and kV feedback signals. Each chip uses a dedicated SPI bus and chip
select line, and both SPI buses operate at 2.25 Mbit/s.

The external ADC module starts a 16-pair burst every 5 ms and acquires both channels using
nonblocking SPI receive DMA transfers. Each received frame is decoded and checked for valid device
framing. At 2 ms after the burst starts, an incomplete or missing result is rejected as an
acquisition-timing error. DMA or framing errors also produce an invalid result and prevent that
result from being used for feedback processing.

The 16 samples from each channel are averaged. The averaged 16-bit codes are converted to engineering
units using the 4.096 V ADC reference and the channel input scaling: a 20 kOhm/10 kOhm divider and
0.1 V/kV for high voltage, and a 30 kOhm/20 kOhm divider and 1 V/mA for cathode current. The paired
result is then published to the feedback and control functions. The ADC chip-select signals remain
inactive while the ADCs are idle.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-10
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-11
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-111
TITLE: External DAC communication
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware communicates with four external DAC IC chips. Each chip has one channel each and are
responsible for setting the grid voltage control signal, filament current control signal, high
voltage control signal, and emission current limit control signal. All DAC chips are identical make
and model, and the firmware communicates with all chips on a singular SPI bus, using independent
chip select signals for each chip. The external DAC module waits for any queued-up requests by the
rest of the firmware to write new DAC values. In case multiple DAC values are queued up simultaneous
or in quick succession, the DAC module processes them in FIFO (first in first out) fashion, waiting
for the SPI bus to be free until the next value is processed.

The DAC module utilizes the manufacturer's hardware abstraction library to write serial data to the
SPI bus. The writes to the SPI bus are non-block interrupt-based calls which allows the processor to
continue operating during the relatively longer SPI transmission (compared to processing speed). The
DAC module utilizes the SPI interrupt handler to set flags for handling the queued DAC requests
(indicating bus is free).

In addition to sending data over the SPI bus, the DAC module handles the four independent digital
chip-select outputs through the hardware abstraction library's GPIO control. All four outputs
initialize inactive high and remain high whenever no DAC transaction is active.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-112
TITLE: Input Debouncing
STATEMENT: >>>
The digital inputs are debounced by sampling the state of the input signals every time a processing
flag gets set. This processing flag is set every 1 msec. Once an input displays a toggled value, the
firmware begins a debounce countdown. During this debounce period, any bouncing signals are ignored.
After the debounce period is over, the firmware checks to see if the input truly did toggle or if it
was just noise. If the signal indicates a 'true' toggle, the firmware updates the changed input
states for processing as well as reporting to the main control application firmware.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-5
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-113
TITLE: Interlock Enable
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware monitors the interlock signals coming from the main control board. These signals can
independently, from the HVPS interface microcontroller, disable emission. However, for redundancy,
the firmware will also disable its own outputs to the HVPS to terminate emission if it detects that
the interlock signals are requesting to disable emission.

Additionally, the firmware will proceed to 'lock' its own emission output controls and disallow
emission until the main control board sends commands to unlock the controls. The unlock sequence
requires that the main control board send these commands while the interlocks are specifically in a
'disabled' state and then toggle the interlocks into an 'enable' state within a certain time period.
This is to ensure that the hardware signals are functioning as the firmware expects and are able to
disable emission if needed. This lock and unlock process is required for both the high voltage
interlock and grid interlock signals coming from the main control board.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-114
TITLE: HVPS Interface Status Flags
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware tracks Boolean status flags in a bitmask reported to the main control board. The
following operating-state flags are monitored and reported:

- HV interlock control enabled
- Grid interlock control enabled
- Filament ramp in progress
- High voltage ramp in progress
- Emission ongoing
- Configuration modification unlocked
- Emission-current controller enabled
- Fast filament ramping enabled

The firmware also reports a distinct latched status flag for each of these HVPS fault sources:

- Filament clock fault
- Cathode arc
- Fan fault
- 24 V overcurrent
- Master fault
- High-voltage overcurrent
- Temperature 1 fault
- Cathode overcurrent
- Temperature 3 fault
- Temperature 2 fault

When a monitored fault input becomes active, the firmware sets the corresponding status flag and
writes a diagnostic entry identifying the fault source and its status and input bit positions.
Deasserting the input does not clear the flag. An explicit fault clear removes the corresponding
latched flag and writes a diagnostic fault-cleared entry.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-5
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-115
TITLE: Output Power
STATEMENT: >>>
The output power is set by the main control board via commands to the firmware. The firmware
uses the power setpoint and high-voltage setpoint to derive the target emission current as power
divided by voltage for the proportional-integral grid controller.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-8
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-116
TITLE: High Voltage Output and Ramping
STATEMENT: >>>
When a target high-voltage value is requested while the high-voltage control is unlocked, the
firmware sets the high-voltage-ramping status and ramps the command toward the target while emission
is inactive. Each 5 ms feedback-control cycle scales the command increment by the elapsed 0.005 s
rather than treating the configured ramp values as per-cycle steps.

The default slow and fast ramp rates are 5 kV/s and 20 kV/s. For either ramp direction, an error
whose magnitude is less than 5 kV uses the slow rate, an error from 5 kV to 20 kV uses four times the
slow rate, and an error of at least 20 kV uses the fast rate. With the defaults, these produce
0.025 kV and 0.1 kV changes per 5 ms cycle. The command is limited to the configured 0 kV to 100 kV
output range.

After the feedback reaches the target, feedback samples within +/-2% of the requested voltage add
5 ms to an elapsed stability accumulator. The firmware clears the high-voltage-ramping status after
the accumulator reaches 4000 ms; samples outside the tolerance do not add to the accumulator. If an
increasing command has reached at least 50 kV and exceeds feedback by more than 50 kV, the firmware
sets the command to zero and cancels the ramp.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-8
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-117
TITLE: Filament Output and Ramping
STATEMENT: >>>
When a target filament-current value is requested, the firmware starts the warming status and
services the ramp every 3 ms while warming is active and emission is inactive. Each active upward
ramp adds 2.25 mA, which is the elapsed-time increment for the fixed 750 mA/s ramp rate. The active
command is limited to the configured minimum and maximum filament-current values. An invalid target
or command outside the configured range sets the target and command to zero and cancels warming.

When feedback has reached the target, each sample from the target through 2% above the target adds
3 ms to the stability accumulator. The firmware clears warming after at least 4000 ms of qualifying
samples. Feedback more than 2% above the target makes the command equal the target without adding
stability time.

While feedback remains below the target, a commanded current at or above the configured minimum that exceeds
feedback by more than 300 mA adds 3 ms to a feedback-discrepancy accumulator. At 6000 ms of
qualifying samples, the firmware sets the filament command to zero and cancels warming.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-6
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-17
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-118
TITLE: Grid Output
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware accepts a direct adjustment-grid command from the main control board and writes values
from zero through the configured maximum to the external grid DAC. While emission-current grid
control is enabled during emission, the feedback controller supersedes the direct command and
updates the grid output within its configured control limits and elapsed-time slew rates.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-7
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-119
TITLE: Emission Limit Output
STATEMENT: >>>
The emission current limit signal is set to a fixed output value
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-9
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-120
TITLE: Emission Current Grid Control
STATEMENT: >>>
Grid control is enabled by default and operates only while x-ray emission is active. The requested
emission current is the requested power divided by the requested high voltage. On activation, the
firmware initializes the adjustment-grid command to its configured maximum of 600 units, clears the
integral state, and runs the proportional-integral calculation from the 5 ms external-feedback
control task.

The controller error is requested cathode current minus measured cathode current. The default
proportional gain is -25 for requested high voltage at or below 50 kV, -35 above 50 kV through
70 kV, and -150 above 70 kV. The integral time is 0.2 s, and each integral increment is calculated
as `(Kp / 0.2 s) * 0.005 s * error`. The firmware compensates the integral state when the gain or
maximum-grid feedforward changes. When the absolute error is within 1% of the absolute requested
current, the controller holds the command and integral state and does not issue a DAC write.

Outside the hold band, the default downward and upward slew limits are 3000 and 1000 grid units per
second, respectively, yielding a reachable range of 15 units down and 5 units up per control cycle.
The command is clamped to both this per-cycle range and the configured 50-to-600-unit output range.
The integral candidate is accepted only when the unsaturated command is inside the reachable range
or when the integral increment unwinds a saturation condition. Disabling grid control or ending
emission restores the maximum-grid command and clears the controller state.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-121
TITLE: DMA Grid Clock
STATEMENT: >>>
The HVPS requires a toggling grid clock signal to allow emission. The firmware uses a timer update
event and DMA to write alternating set and reset values to the grid-clock GPIO output. This produces
the grid-clock waveform without a timer interrupt handler.

Enabling the clock resets the output low, initializes the DMA transfer, and starts the timer.
Disabling the clock stops the timer and DMA transfer and leaves the output low.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-1
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-122
TITLE: External Feedback Control Task
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware creates a dedicated FreeRTOS task for external ADC result processing and feedback
control. The periodic external ADC completion check notifies the task once per 5 ms acquisition
period. A valid result replaces the high-voltage and cathode-current feedback values; invalid
results do not replace feedback. Each notification then processes the high-voltage ramp and
emission-current grid controller using the current feedback.

High-voltage increments, grid-controller integration, and grid-command slew are each multiplied by
the 0.005 s control period. Their behavior therefore follows configured rates and time constants
rather than a fixed change per task invocation. Shared status and controller state are protected
while accessed by the task and interrupt context. DAC writes are performed outside the protected
state-processing region so external feedback processing does not block communication, I/O, or DAC
servicing.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-10
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-11
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-35
TITLE: Manufacturer Hardware Abstraction
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware integrates source code provided by the manufacturer of the microcontroller unit to
interface with various peripherals of the microcontroller. These include functions to interface with
the following peripherals of the microcontroller:

- UART serial port
- GPIO
- SPI serial port
- Flash memory controller
- ADC peripheral
- Timer peripheral
<<<
