[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-36
TITLE: Input Signals
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware logs several digital input signals coming from the HVPS and monitors them to determine
whether an HVPS fault has occurred. OC_CAT is handled separately from the active-high fault inputs:
a low level on the initial input sample and each subsequent high-to-low transition reports the
cathode-overcurrent fault.

The firmware also monitors three digital inputs from the main control board for enabling and
disabling emission: HV interlock, grid interlock, and the emission start signal.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-5
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-37
TITLE: Output Signals
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware controls various digital output signals. There are grid clocks and watchdog reset
signals which are toggled periodically by the firmware. Additionally, there are three digital
control signals which disable output on the HVPS: grid allowed, PFC allowed, and HV allowed. These
three signals correspond to three signals on the HVPS which similarly are used to disallow grid
switching, PFC enabling, and HV enabling on the HVPS. All these signals need to be appropriately
asserted for emission output to be enabled.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-123
TITLE: Logging Module
STATEMENT: >>>
The compile-time logging switch defaults to enabled with DEBUG as the highest included severity.
Initialization binds output to USART3, enables the runtime switch, and clears a 1024-byte transmit ring
buffer. ERROR, WARN, INFO, and DEBUG calls format severity-tagged records in a 256-byte working buffer,
truncate oversized records to leave room for a CRLF terminator, and enqueue them for FTDI_TX. Startup
emits an INFO record containing `Sensus setup complete`. Unprefixed formatted output and newline-delimited
JSON status containing `runtime_ms`, `sys_stat`, and `sys_io_bits` use the same queue and runtime switch;
the optional 500 ms periodic status task is disabled by default. The UART interrupt starts one-byte
transmission and each transmit-complete callback drains the next queued byte. Writers briefly mask
interrupts while copying bytes; if the ring buffer fills, they drop the remainder instead of waiting.
Runtime-disabled logging returns before formatting or enqueueing output. The USART3 error callback
clears UART error flags so subsequent transmission can proceed.

After input debouncing identifies a transition, the HV interlock handler emits WARN records
containing `Hv int rose` or `Hv int fell`, the grid interlock handler emits `Grid int rose`
or `Grid int fell`, and the beam-control handler emits `Beam ctrl rose` or
`Beam ctrl fell`.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-18
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-124
TITLE: GET and SET Configurations Command Processing
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware recognizes an 11-byte GET frame consisting of the `*GET..` command, a little-endian
16-bit parameter-category identifier, a little-endian 16-bit parameter index, and a newline
terminator. For a supported configuration-value category and index, GET reads the selected value and
returns a 15-byte response containing `*GET..`, the received category and index, the value as a
little-endian IEEE-754 32-bit float, and a newline terminator. The firmware recognizes a 15-byte SET
frame consisting of the `*..SET` command, the same category and index fields, a little-endian
IEEE-754 32-bit float value, and a newline terminator. A supported SET updates the selected
configuration value and returns the corresponding 15-byte `*..SET` confirmation frame. Requests
with an unrecognized command header or unsupported category, SET requests outside the configuration
table, and requests with an index of 64 or greater are ignored without response.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-19
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-125
TITLE: All-Values Bulk Read Command Processing
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware recognizes the seven-byte `*ACFGS\n`, `*ASPTS\n`, and `*AFBVS\n` bulk-read requests
by their five-character command and expected frame length. In one response, the firmware echoes
`*` and the request command, appends every value from the requested category as consecutive
little-endian IEEE-754 32-bit floats in increasing index order, and appends a newline terminator.
`ACFGS` returns all 32 configuration values in a 135-byte response, `ASPTS` returns all six
setpoints in a 31-byte response, and `AFBVS` returns all five feedback values in a 27-byte
response. An unrecognized command header is discarded without response.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-19
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-126
TITLE: Firmware Version Response
STATEMENT: >>>
The control-command processor recognizes command value ``0x7F`` as a version request. It
queues a 13-word UART response containing two synchronization words, the ``VRSN`` marker,
the operating-mode identifier, the null-padded release version string in a 32-byte field,
and an additive checksum. The response is sent when the UART transmitter is available,
ahead of the next periodic telemetry frame.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-20
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-127
TITLE: Development-build boot validation marker
STATEMENT: >>>
During FTDI setup in builds where ``RELEASE`` is not defined, the firmware reads the application CRC
location. If it already contains ``0xDEADBEEF``, no flash operation is performed. Otherwise, the
firmware erases the CRC flash page when the location is not erased, programs ``0xDEADBEEF``, and
verifies the programmed value. Builds where ``RELEASE`` is defined skip this operation.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-21
  ROLE: Refines

